{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Patching" heading="See what is out of date, then patch it safely" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Scan Linux nodes for the updates they are missing, and apply them in
    batches with a way to stop before a bad update reaches the whole fleet.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Pending updates, as each node's package manager reports them</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      A scan asks the node's agent to run apt, dnf or yum in a mode that
      changes nothing, then shows each pending update with its installed
      and available versions and whether the package manager calls it a
      security update. A node also says whether it already needs a reboot.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Scans run on a schedule you control, or on demand for one node or a
      whole group. A node that cannot be scanned is listed as unable to
      report &mdash; never shown as fully patched.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "patching"}}

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Patch in batches, with a safety stop</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Choose security updates, everything, or named packages, and how many
      nodes to patch at once. Set how many failures end the run, so that no
      further batch starts once something has gone wrong. Preview with a
      dry run that installs nothing, and let nodes that need it reboot when
      they are done.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Progress is shown node by node, a node that goes quiet part-way is
      reported as having done so instead of hanging forever, and when it
      finishes the node is scanned again so the page shows what is really
      left.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-timeline>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Scan" data-i18n-attr="heading">Each node reports what its package manager would upgrade.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Dry run" data-i18n-attr="heading">Preview a run on the nodes you chose. Nothing is installed.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Batches" data-i18n-attr="heading">Patch a few nodes at a time, stopping after as many failures as you allow.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Rescan" data-i18n-attr="heading">Each patched node is scanned again, so what remains is on record.</vox-timeline-item>
</vox-timeline>

<vox-grid min="240px" gap="lg">
  <vox-card heading="Scoped to your nodes" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="node-group"></vox-icon>
    Scanning and patching follow the same node group access as every other
    action: people can patch the nodes their roles cover, and no others.
  </vox-card>
  <vox-card heading="Scheduled or on demand" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="orchestrate"></vox-icon>
    Patch runs are jobs like any other, with results per node, and show up
    in the job history with their options.
  </vox-card>
</vox-grid>

<vox-callout variant="warning" heading="Linux with apt, dnf or yum" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    Windows, macOS and zypper (SUSE) nodes are not scanned. They are listed
    as unable to report, so a gap in coverage is visible rather than
    mistaken for a clean bill of health.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
